  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n3x{y}^{\u2033}+{y}^{\u2032}-4y=0\n$$\n\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the missing coefficient in the first solution ${y}_{1}?$\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE, the indicial equation of the ODE is $3{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda =0,$ and the recurrence relation for the coefficients is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}(\\lambda )=\\frac{4}{(\\lambda +n)(3(\\lambda +n)-2)}\\,{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\n*\n\nThe first solution is \n\n${y}_{1}(x)={a}_{0}({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{5/3}\\,+$ {{field-1}} ${x}^{8/3}+\u22ef).$",
